<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East Frisia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ostfriesland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> is a coastal region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between the Dollart and the Jade, including the East Frisian Islands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was part of the Frisian Freedom, governed by local chieftains, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> family united it as an imperial county in 1464. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1744 and to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-frisia.jpg" alt="East Frisia" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Onno · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostfriesland_hervorgehoben.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> lies in the north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, mainly on the western half of the East Frisian peninsula. It consists of the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>, Leer and Wittmund and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>. Off the coast lies a chain of islands, from west to east Borkum, Juist, Norderney, Baltrum, Langeoog and Spiekeroog, stretching for 90 kilometres. The tide leaves a broad stretch of mudflat between the islands and the mainland, which is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. Inland the land is largely Geest and heath. The first proven historical event is the arrival of a Roman fleet under Drusus in 12 BCE, which sailed up the Ems and returned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Chauci, mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, lived between the Ems and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> until Frisian expansion after about 500 displaced them in part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> also settled, so that the medieval population mixes Frisian and Saxon elements. A Frisian kingdom under kings such as Radbod was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a> in 689, and the region became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a>. Charles the Great divided it into two counties, and the missionaries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludger">Liudger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willehad">Willehad</a> began their work there. After the Carolingian empire decayed, independent districts elected councillors called Redjeven each year, who were both judges and administrators. This prevented feudalism and gave the period its name, the Frisian freedom. Representatives of the districts met yearly at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upstalsboom">Upstalsboom</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>.</p><p>In the 14th century the Redjeven system decayed amid plague and other disasters, and chieftain families took control of villages and regions. The County of Oldenburg tried several times to subdue East Frisia, and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> failed in 1156. In 1400 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> forced the chieftains to stop sheltering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker">Klaus Störtebeker</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> family of Greetsiel later defeated rival clans, and one of them was made a count by Emperor Frederick III. In 1517 the emperor accepted Count Edzard and his descendants after Edzard had resisted an order to name a duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> as heir. Menno Simons found refuge there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The counts of East Frisia were raised to princes in 1654. East Frisia passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1744 when the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> prince died without an heir, and was annexed in 1806 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a> and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>, under which family names were introduced in 1811. After 1813 it returned to Prussia, which in 1815 ceded it to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, itself annexed by Prussia in 1866. East Frisia has about 469,000 inhabitants. Most of its people are Protestant, Reformed around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a> and Lutheran at Leer, Norden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>, and East Frisia is noted for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> culture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “East Frisia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a century of feuds among Frisian chieftains, Ulrich Cirksena united most of <b>East Frisia</b>, and in 1464 Emperor Frederick III made him an imperial count.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-east-frisia">County of East Frisia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>East Frisia</b> came under the Cirksena counts in 1464, and in 1498 Emperor Maximilian made Albert of Saxony governor of Friesland, ending the freedom west of the Ems and creating the Lordship of Frisia.</blockquote><a href="/state/frisian-freedom">Frisian Freedom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna raised the Electorate of Hanover to a kingdom in 1814, with George III as king, and enlarged it with <b>East Frisia</b>, Hildesheim and Lingen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a></li><li><blockquote>Mary of Jever, who ruled from 1517 to 1575, defended her small land against <b>East Frisia</b> by placing it under Habsburg protection as a fief of Brabant.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-jever">Lordship of Jever</a></li></ul></section>
